#import "../template.typ": *

#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Glossary of climbing jargon] <glossary>

Plain definitions of the slang and technical words used in this book. Technique names have full entries in Part II; here they get one line and a link. Words with a #underline(stroke: (thickness: 0.5pt, dash: "dotted"), offset: 2pt)[dotted underline] in the text lead here.

#set par(justify: false)
#columns(2, gutter: 1.6em)[

#gloss("ACL")[The anterior cruciate ligament, the main ligament inside the knee that stops the shin sliding forward.]

#gloss("ape index")[Your arm span minus your height. A positive ape index means your arms are long for your height.]

#gloss("arête", see: ("arete",))[An outside corner of the wall, like the edge of a building.]

#gloss("Babyboxer")[A tucked landing position taught by the German Alpine Club: knees bent, arms bent in front of you, chin tucked.]

#gloss("bail")[To let go on purpose when a move fails, so you choose how you land.]

#gloss("barn door", see: ("barn-door",))[Swinging off the wall like a door on its hinges, because the hand and foot holding you are on the same side.]

#gloss("base of support")[The holds that are carrying you: footholds, handholds, a heel or a knee. Your weight is stable when it sits over them.]

#gloss("beta")[The sequence of moves and body positions that gets you up a problem. "Giving beta" means telling someone how to do it.]

#gloss("BMI")[Body mass index: your weight in kilograms divided by your height in metres squared.]

#gloss("body tension", see: ("body-tension",))[Using the muscles of your trunk, hips and legs to keep your feet pressed on the wall, especially on overhangs.]

#gloss("campus", see: ("campus-move",))[Climbing with your hands only, feet off the wall.]

#gloss("campus board")[A training board of wooden rungs that you climb with your hands only.]

#gloss("centre of gravity")[The point your weight acts through. Standing upright, it sits in your pelvis, near your navel. It moves when you reach or raise a leg.]

#gloss("circuit")[A set of problems marked with one colour, covering one grade or a range of two or three. Many gyms use circuits instead of grading each problem.]

#gloss("collagen")[The protein that tendons, ligaments and pulleys are made of. It adapts to training more slowly than muscle.]

#gloss("comp style")[Setting inspired by competitions: big volumes, jumps, run-and-jump starts and coordination moves on modern holds.]

#gloss("contact strength")[How hard you can grab a hold in the first split second, e.g. as you catch it at the end of a dynamic move.]

#gloss("coordination move")[A move that needs several limbs timed together, often with a jump or a run. Also called a coordination dyno.]

#gloss("core", see: ("body-tension",))[The muscles around your trunk (abdominals, back and hips) that connect your hands to your feet.]

#gloss("crimp", see: ("half-crimp", "full-crimp"))[A small edge you hold on your fingertips. Also the grip itself: crimping means holding with your finger joints bent sharply.]

#gloss("crux")[The hardest move or section of a problem.]

#gloss("cut loose", see: ("cutting-loose",))[When your feet come off the wall and swing, deliberately or not, leaving you hanging on your hands.]

#gloss("DAV")[The German Alpine Club (Deutscher Alpenverein), which publishes climbing safety guidance and gym accident statistics.]

#gloss("deadpoint", see: ("deadpoint",))[The top of a movement, when your body is momentarily weightless. Grabbing the next hold at that instant takes the least strength.]

#gloss("deliberate practice")[Practice aimed at a specific weakness, with feedback, rather than just climbing more.]

#gloss("downclimb")[Climbing back down instead of jumping off. It is the safest way off a problem.]

#gloss("downturned shoe")[A climbing shoe with the toe curled down. It helps you pull with your toes on overhangs; flat shoes are better on slabs.]

#gloss("dual texture")[Another name for dual-tex.]

#gloss("dual-tex")[A hold or volume with a rough zone to grip or stand on and a smooth zone that is meant to be hard to use.]

#gloss("dynamic")[A move that uses momentum, so you can't stop halfway. The opposite of static.]

#gloss("dyno", see: ("dyno",))[A jump from one set of holds to another, with both feet leaving the wall. You catch with one or both hands.]

#gloss("edge", see: ("edging",))[A flat-topped hold with a squarish lip, gripped with the fingertips; also, standing on a foothold with the edge of your shoe.]

#gloss("external focus")[Attending to the effect of a movement (e.g. "push the wall away") rather than to your body. It usually helps learning.]

#gloss("feature")[A large shape bolted to the wall to imitate rock, such as a bulge or a fin. Unlike holds, features usually count for every problem.]

#gloss("fingerboard")[Another name for a hangboard.]

#gloss("flag", see: ("flag",))[Pressing a free leg against the wall, without a hold, to balance and stop yourself rotating.]

#gloss("flake")[A thin blade of rock, or a hold shaped like one, standing off the wall so you can pull sideways on its edge.]

#gloss("flash")[Sending a problem on your first attempt, after watching others or getting beta. Doing it with no information at all is an onsight.]

#gloss("Font grade")[The Fontainebleau grading scale (3, 4, 5, 6A, 6A+ …), used in many European gyms. The Grade conversion appendix converts it to V-grades.]

#gloss("Font scale")[Another name for Font grade.]

#gloss("forerun")[The setters' test climb of a new problem before it opens, to check the moves and set the grade.]

#gloss("four-point start")[A start where each hand and each foot has its own marked start hold.]

#gloss("friction")[The grip between your skin or shoe rubber and a surface. Slopers, smears and volumes rely on it; cool, dry conditions increase it.]

#gloss("gaston", see: ("gaston",))[A hold you pull outward, away from your body, with your elbow out and thumb down, like prising open lift doors.]

#gloss("hamstring")[The muscles at the back of the thigh. They bend the knee and pull in a heel hook.]

#gloss("hangboard")[A board of edges and pockets for hanging from, used to train finger strength. Also called a fingerboard.]

#gloss("heel hook", see: ("heel-hook",))[Hooking your heel on a hold and pulling with it, as if it were a third hand.]

#gloss("hinge", see: ("barn-door",))[The line between the hand and foot that are holding you. If your weight is off to one side of it, your body rotates around it like a door.]

#gloss("incut")[A hold whose gripping surface slopes back into the wall, so your fingers lock behind its lip.]

#gloss("intermediate", see: ("bumping",))[A small hold between two better ones, used briefly to get from one to the other.]

#gloss("jerk")[How abruptly your body speeds up or slows down. Smooth movement keeps it low and spares your fingers.]

#gloss("jug", see: ("jug",))[A large hold you can wrap your whole hand around, like a jug handle.]

#gloss("LCL")[The lateral collateral ligament, on the outside of the knee. Hard heel hooks that twist the knee load it.]

#gloss("ligament")[A tough band of tissue that holds the bones of a joint together.]

#gloss("link-up")[Climbing a section, or the whole problem, in one go after practising its moves separately. "Linking" a problem means climbing it in one attempt.]

#gloss("lip")[The top edge of a wall, roof or volume, where you move from steep ground onto easier ground. Also the front edge of a hold.]

#gloss("lock-off", see: ("lock-off",))[Holding a hold with your arm bent and still, so the other hand can reach.]

#gloss("lumbrical")[One of the small muscles in the palm attached to the finger tendons. It can tear when neighbouring fingers are bent very differently, as in some pockets.]

#gloss("mantle", see: ("mantle",))[Pressing down on a ledge or the top of a hold to push your body up above it, like climbing out of a swimming pool.]

#gloss("marker hold")[A memorable hold you use to anchor your memory of a sequence ("after the blue pinch, cross through").]

#gloss("match", see: ("matching",))[Putting both hands (or both feet) on the same hold.]

#gloss("max hang")[The most extra weight you can hang from a given edge for a few seconds. Coaches use it to measure finger strength.]

#gloss("MCL")[The medial collateral ligament, on the inside of the knee. A deep drop knee can strain it, especially when you're tired.]

#gloss("meniscus")[A rubbery pad of cartilage inside the knee. Twisting on a bent knee, as in a deep drop knee, can tear it.]

#gloss("micro beta")[Small personal adjustments to a sequence: exactly where a finger or toe goes, or which way a hand is turned.]

#gloss("momentum")[The speed and direction your body carries. A swing or a jump builds it; you use it to reach a hold you couldn't reach statically.]

#gloss("negative hold")[A hold whose surface slopes away from you, so it pushes your fingers off. The opposite of incut.]

#gloss("off foot")[The foot that is not carrying you. You can flag it or press it on the wall to stop rotation.]

#gloss("onsight")[Sending a problem on your first attempt without watching anyone or getting any beta.]

#gloss("open hand", see: ("open-hand",))[Gripping with your fingers only slightly bent, letting the hold sit in the first finger joints. It is the safest grip for the fingers.]

#gloss("overhang", see: ("overhang",))[A wall that leans out over you, steeper than vertical. Also called steep ground.]

#gloss("palmar plate")[A thick ligament on the palm side of each finger joint. It can be strained by crimping or by forcing a finger straight.]

#gloss("parkour")[The sport of running, jumping and swinging over obstacles. Comp-style setting borrows many of its moves.]

#gloss("pinch", see: ("pinch",))[A hold you squeeze between your thumb and fingers.]

#gloss("plumb line")[An imaginary line hanging straight down from your centre of gravity. It shows where your weight goes.]

#gloss("pocket", see: ("pocket",))[A hole in a hold that fits one to three fingers.]

#gloss("positive hold")[A hold that is easy to pull on in the direction you need: its gripping surface faces up or tilts back into the wall, rather than sloping away.]

#gloss("problem")[A boulder route: a set of holds, with a start and a finish, that you climb without ropes.]

#gloss("project")[A problem at your limit that you work on over several attempts or sessions. "Projecting" means working on one.]

#gloss("pulley")[One of the bands of tissue that hold the finger tendons against the bones. Pulley strains and tears are the most common overuse injury in climbing.]

#gloss("pump")[The tight, burning feeling in your forearms when the muscles tire faster than blood can clear the waste.]

#gloss("pumped")[Tired and swollen in the forearms, with a fading grip. See pump.]

#gloss("rail")[A long horizontal hold or edge that you can move your hands along.]

#gloss("RED-S")[Relative energy deficiency in sport: what happens when you eat too little for the training you do.]

#gloss("roof", see: ("roof",))[A section of wall that is close to horizontal, so you climb it facing the floor.]

#gloss("sandbagged")[Harder than its grade suggests.]

#gloss("scrunchy")[Describes holds set close together, so you have to fold up small. Hard for tall climbers.]

#gloss("send")[To climb a problem from start to finish without falling. Also the successful attempt itself.]

#gloss("setter")[The person who designs and bolts the problems in a gym. Also called a route setter.]

#gloss("shake out")[Resting one arm by letting it hang and shaking it loose while you hold on with the other.]

#gloss("shoulder impingement")[Pain when a tendon in the shoulder gets pinched under the bony roof of the shoulder blade, often from overhead pulling.]

#gloss("sidepull", see: ("sidepull",))[A hold you pull sideways towards your body, leaning away from it.]

#gloss("sit start", see: ("sit-start",))[A start from sitting on the mat, with your bottom on the floor and hands on the start holds.]

#gloss("slab", see: ("slab",))[A wall that leans back, less steep than vertical. You climb it mostly on your feet.]

#gloss("SLAP tear")[A tear in the rim of cartilage around the shoulder socket, where the biceps tendon attaches.]

#gloss("sloper", see: ("sloper",))[A rounded hold with no edge, held by friction and by keeping your body below it.]

#gloss("smear", see: ("smearing",))[Pressing the sole of your shoe flat against the wall or a volume where there is no foothold, holding on friction.]

#gloss("soft")[Easier than its grade suggests. The opposite of sandbagged.]

#gloss("spin-out")[Rotating out of a backstep because the foot is behind the line. A drop knee is often the fix.]

#gloss("spot")[Another name for spotting.]

#gloss("spotter")[The person who is spotting. See spotting.]

#gloss("spotting")[Guiding a falling climber's upper body so they land on their feet on the mat.]

#gloss("start holds")[The holds you must start on, usually marked with tape or a tag. "Hands on, feet on" means both hands start on the marked holds and your feet leave the floor.]

#gloss("static")[A move done slowly enough that you could stop and hold any position along the way. The opposite of dynamic.]

#gloss("sweet spot")[The best part of a hold: the deepest, most incut or best-textured spot. Find it before you weight the hold.]

#gloss("tendon")[A cord of tissue that joins a muscle to a bone.]

#gloss("tendon glides")[A sequence of finger positions that slides the finger tendons through their full range; a common warm-up.]

#gloss("the line")[The vertical line straight down from the hold your holding hand pulls on. Feet placed on it, or just past it away from your reach, keep you from rotating.]

#gloss("top out", see: ("top-out",))[Finishing a problem by climbing onto the top of the wall or boulder instead of holding a finishing hold.]

#gloss("training board")[A standard steep wall with a fixed grid of holds, such as the MoonBoard, Kilter Board or Tension Board, with problems shared through an app.]

#gloss("undercling", see: ("undercling",))[A hold that faces down, so you pull it upward from below.]

#gloss("variable practice")[Practising a movement with small changes each time (different holds, angles or beta) so it transfers to new problems.]

#gloss("volume", see: ("volume",))[A big hollow shape bolted to the wall, usually a pyramid or wedge. You can stand on it, press it or grip its edges.]

#gloss("V-scale")[The American boulder grading scale, from VB (beginner) and V0 upward. V5 is roughly Font 6C.]

#gloss("wall angle")[How steep the wall is: slab (leaning back), vertical, overhang (leaning out) or roof (near horizontal).]
]
